      "data": {
        "text": "**Defining the Query Source**\n\nI've decided to use Google Cloud Monitoring as the data provider. Now, my focus is solidifying the file structure for `query_metric_window.py` and ensuring that the necessary Google Cloud credentials and setup are appropriately integrated and identifiable within the code.\n\n\n**Constructing the Core Query**\n\nOkay, I'm now actively building out `query_metric_window.py`. I've outlined the core logic: importing `google.cloud.monitoring_v3`, establishing a Monitoring API client, and designing a function to handle the metric data query. It will require the project ID, metric type, and a time window as parameters, then call `list_time_series`. Let's get coding!\n\n\n",
        "time": {
          "end": 1789864749996,
          "start": 1789864746835
        },
        "type": "reasoning"
      },
      "id": "prt_0bc409f53001Lxr7NkYuuKO3By",
      "message_id": "msg_0bc4099d800142JX6Cy5wKXppW",
      "session_id": "ses_f43bf66a8ffeIazy8N51B3UEoj",
      "time_created": 1789864746835,
      "time_updated": 1789864749997
    },
    {
      "data": {
        "callID": "VAvlNuRAj3qygScy",
        "metadata": {
          "google": {
            "thoughtSignature": ""
          }
        },
        "state": {
          "input": {
            "content": "\nfrom google.cloud import monitoring_v3\nfrom google.protobuf import timestamp_pb2\nimport time\n\ndef query_metric_window(project_id: str, metric_type: str, minutes: int = 5):\n    \"\"\"Queries recent datapoints for a named metric over a time window using Google Cloud Monitoring.\n\n    Args:\n        project_id: Your Google Cloud project ID.\n        metric_type: The type of metric to query (e.g., \"compute.googleapis.com/instance/cpu/utilization\").\n        minutes: The number of minutes to query data for (default 5 minutes).\n    \"\"\"\n    client = monitoring_v3.MetricServiceClient()\n    project_name = f\"projects/{project_id}\"\n\n    now = time.time()\n    seconds = int(now)\n    nanos = int((now - seconds) * 10**9)\n\n    interval = monitoring_v3.TimeInterval(\n        end_time=timestamp_pb2.Timestamp(seconds=seconds, nanos=nanos),\n        start_time=timestamp_pb2.Timestamp(seconds=(seconds - minutes * 60), nanos=nanos),\n    )\n\n    query_filter = f'metric.type = \"{metric_type}\"'\n\n    results = client.list_time_series(\n        request={\n            \"name\": project_name,\n            \"filter\": query_filter,\n            \"interval\": interval,\n            \"view\": monitoring_v3.ListTimeSeriesRequest.TimeSeriesView.FULL,\n        }\n    )\n\n    for series in results:\n        print(f\"Metric: {series.metric.type_}\")\n        for point in series.points:\n            print(f\"  Time: {point.interval.end_time.ToDatetime()}, Value: {point.value.double_value}\")\n\nif __name__ == '__main__':\n    # Replace 'your-project-id' with your actual Google Cloud Project ID\n    # Replace 'compute.googleapis.com/instance/cpu/utilization' with the desired metric type\n    query_metric_window(\"your-project-id\", \"compute.googleapis.com/instance/cpu/utilization\", 10)\n",
            "filePath": "query_metric_window.py"
          },
